{"attack": true,"case": "The target's mechanism asserts that preferring the best-corroborated theory is 'the only rational move available,' yet the target's own claim concludes this preference is 'a pragmatic gamble without epistemic justification.' These are in direct tension. The target provides no account of what species of rationality would make the move 'rational' once epistemic justification has been stripped away. Popper's 'critical rationality' is an epistemic notion — it concerns how we acquire and revise knowledge. If the target grants (as it does) that the preference carries no epistemic justification, then labeling it 'rational' in the mechanism is an unsupported terminological move: rule-following without any justification (epistemic, instrumental, or otherwise) is convention or habit, not rationality. The target never bridges this gap, so its mechanism smuggles in a normative conclusion ('rational') that its own claim has explicitly disarmed.","premise": "A move is 'rational' only if there is some identifiable form of justification (epistemic, instrumental, or otherwise) warranting it; mere adherence to a rule that has survived criticism, with epistemic justification explicitly disclaimed, is not sufficient for rationality.","premise_evidence": [{"block": "adbff8f6410a3283e1a19240e1b8b8503a4f55c93481ff1edd6eab40e3f06ec5", "quote": "Choosing the one that hasn't failed yet is the only rational move available, even if it offers no guarantee of future success."}, {"block": "adbff8f6410a3283e1a19240e1b8b8503a4f55c93481ff1edd6eab40e3f06ec5", "quote": "reducing the preference to a pragmatic gamble without epistemic justification"}],"successor_question": "What non-epistemic conception of rationality, if any, could license calling a methodological rule 'rational' once the target has conceded that following it provides no epistemic justification for expecting future success?"}